## Claim
Text: "Even if your mail-in ballot is postmarked prior to the Election day but arrives after the Election day it will be thrown out."
Claim author: Elizabeth Gilbert
Claim date: October 28, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201029195951/https://www.facebook.com/GilbertLiz/photos/a.356148997800555/3549940058421417/

## Initial Q&A
### For the 2020 United States general election, did any state require that mail-in ballots be received by Election Day (November 3, 2020) to be counted, regardless of the postmark date?
Answer: Yes, according to the source, Wisconsin required that mail-in ballots be received by Election Day (November 3, 2020) to be counted, regardless of postmark date. The article states: "Wisconsin, meanwhile, will only accept ballots received by Election Day, after the Supreme Court ruled 5–3 this week to block a lower court judge who ordered an extension."

Source URL: https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/addybaird/vote-by-mail-state-deadlines

### Did any state in the 2020 US general election have a law that counted mail-in ballots postmarked on or before Election Day even if they arrived after Election Day?
Answer: Yes, multiple states had laws that counted mail-in ballots postmarked on or before Election Day even if they arrived after Election Day. For example, Ohio required ballots to be postmarked by November 2 but allowed them to arrive as late as November 13 and still be counted. Washington state allowed ballots received through November 23, and Pennsylvania accepted ballots through November 6. The article notes: "One state, Ohio, requires that ballots be postmarked by Nov. 2, but they can arrive as late as the close of business on Nov. 13... and still be counted." Additionally, "Washington state... has the latest deadline, allowing ballots received through Nov. 23 to be counted."

Source URL: https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/addybaird/vote-by-mail-state-deadlines

